dotnet_build_calls": 0, "edit_calls": 2, "ended_on_rejection": false, "error": null, "exec_mode": "local", "failure_class": "solved", "failure_reason": null, "faults_served": 0, "final_answer_complete": true, "final_answer_present": true, "final_answer_source": "json", "final_workspace": "/workspace/openbench-ab/results/omp-cap30-b2/cap30-44/omp-44/cells/with/trig-omp-multistep/27/workspace", "fresh_subagents": 0, "harness": "omp", "harness_version": "omp/18.3.0 (/workspace/pi-check/cache5/bin/omp/d3e08e91b65a46fc6d88bbb8ba641b6047172ce5/omp)", "harness_version_source": "host", "host": "grok-bot-vm-412615941", "image_digest": null, "last_activity_age_s": null, "list_has_generated": null, "main_agent_searched": false, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "model_context_window": 1000000, "model_max_tokens": 128000, "output_tail": "Fixed. The bug was in `ledger/currency.py`, not the totals code: `to_cents` converted prices with `int(amount * 100)`, which drops the fraction instead of rounding. Floats like `19.99` and `0.29` sit just below their true value, so every such price lost one cent:\n\n- `19.99 * 100` came out as `1998.999\u2026` \u2192 1998 cents instead of 1999\n- `0.29 * 100` \u2192 28 instead of 29, so 3 of them came to 84 instead of 87\n- `4.35 * 100` \u2192 434 instead of 435, which threw off the discounted total\n\n**Change:** `to_cents` now turns the float into a `Decimal` via `str(amount)` and rounds to whole cents, with halves rounding up. That matches the half-up rounding already used in `discounts.py` and `tax.py`.\n\n**Verification:** pytest isn't installed here, so I called every test function in `tests/test_totals.py` and `tests/test_report.py` directly; all 5 passed. Spot checks: `to_cents(19.99)` gives 1999, `to_cents(0.125)` gives 13 and `to_cents(-4.35)` gives -435.", "paced_wait_s": 0.0, "permission_rejections": 0, "read_calls": 0, "replies_ok": 5, "replies_throttled": 0, "requests_cache_read": 46850, "requests_cache_write": 16119, "requests_cost_usd